<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    pms-oracle — the Oracle PMS connector.

    # Why `Microsoft.NET.Sdk` and not `Sdk.Web`, today

    The manifest declares `runtime.kind: dotnet-service`, and this will be a
    hosted service. It is a library right now because **the surface it would
    host does not exist**: the Integration Hub is designed (platform brief
    §7–§20) and unbuilt, `shared/protos/hotelos/integration/` carries no
    contract, and there is no `integration-service` for this process to
    register with.

    What *is* buildable today is everything ADR 0128 §5 makes the connector's
    own — "the connector supplies protocol and business mapping: endpoints, the
    authentication adapter, request/response mapping, the status parser,
    normalisation rules, capability declaration". None of that needs the Hub to
    exist; all of it is wrong to invent twice.

    So this project holds the connector's own knowledge, and the host arrives
    with the contract it would serve. That is a one-line SDK change and a
    `Program.cs`, not a restructure.

    # What it deliberately does not reference

    No `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore`, no `Npgsql`. The connector owns no
    schema — the Hub owns the `integration` schema and Master Data owns rooms —
    and the manifest carries no `database:` block. A package that referenced an
    ORM would be a package that had somewhere to put a `property` table, which
    is the reference system's nine-copies defect (design §2.7).
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net9.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>PmsOracle</RootNamespace>
    <!--
      Fixed, because the manifest names it: `runtime.assembly` is
      `PmsOracle.Connector.dll`. If the two drift, an install fails with a
      file-not-found that reads like a broken package.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>PmsOracle.Connector</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- ADR 0027: the build is the lint. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <!-- ADR 0057: every public member is documented, and the build says so. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      Framework-dependent — ADR 0090 §Q23: the platform chains the runtime, so
      a package carries its own assemblies and not a copy of .NET.
    -->
    <SelfContained>false</SelfContained>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The platform's normalised-fact contract — `hotelos/integration/v1/dto.proto`.

    # Why a sibling path

    The application repository's charter permits two forms while the SDK is
    unpublished: "a sibling-path reference to the platform checkout, or a copied
    `shared/protos` — an implementation choice for now, until an SDK-publishing
    round makes them proper artifacts (ADR 0122 addendum)".

    Of the two, the path. **A copy drifts silently; a path fails loudly when the
    checkout is absent**, and loud beats silent everywhere in this platform —
    the same reasoning that puts an unrecognised status value into a rejected
    record instead of a null.

    # Interim, and it dies with the SDK-publishing round

    Noted here the way `manifest.yaml` notes its own pending half. This
    `..\..\..\HosPilotOS\` assumes the two repositories are checked out beside
    each other, which is true of every machine today and is not a contract.
    When the SDK ships as an artifact this ItemGroup becomes a PackageReference
    and the assumption goes with it.

    `GrpcServices="None"`: the connector reads and writes these messages. It
    serves no gRPC of its own, and the Hub's service surface does not exist yet.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Protobuf Include="..\..\..\HosPilotOS\shared\protos\hotelos\integration\v1\dto.proto"
              ProtoRoot="..\..\..\HosPilotOS\shared\protos"
              GrpcServices="None" />
    <Protobuf Include="..\..\..\HosPilotOS\shared\protos\hotelos\common\v1\common.proto"
              ProtoRoot="..\..\..\HosPilotOS\shared\protos"
              GrpcServices="None" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Google.Protobuf" Version="3.29.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="Grpc.Tools" Version="2.71.0">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
